## Why This Is Asked
Tests crossing-boundary bit extraction on a 128-bit value with no branching, mirroring real-world crypto/low-level data parsing tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-boundary bit manipulation.
- Mask generation for widths up to 64 without 64-bit overflow.
- Handling 128-bit values via hi/lo halves (no loops).

## Code Example
```javascript
function extractBits128(hi, lo, pos, width) {
  const V = (BigInt(hi) << 64n) | BigInt(lo);
  const mask = width === 64 ? (1n << 64n) - 1n : (1n << BigInt(width)) - 1n;
  return (V >> BigInt(pos)) & mask;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

- Tests ability to implement a canonical bit-counting trick without loops, emphasizing constant-time operations. 
- Demonstrates careful use of masks and shifts to aggregate counts across bit groups.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (parallel bit counting)
- Bit masks 0x55555555, 0x33333333, 0x0F0F0F0F
- Constant-time arithmetic with shifts

## Code Example

```javascript
function popcount32(x) {
  x = x - ((x >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x + (x >>> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  return (x * 0x01010101) >>> 24;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to 64-bit inputs?
- Compare this approach to using hardware popcount instructions in terms of portability and performance.

## Why This Is Asked

New angle: checks two-bit pair occurrences across 128 bits without branches, emphasizing careful cross-half packing.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit lane partitioning; - mask-based pair extraction; - correct half packing of results.

## Code Example

```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 twoBitsSetMask128(uint128 x){
  const uint64_t M = 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  uint64_t loMask = (x.lo & M) & ((x.lo >> 1) & M);
  uint64_t hiMask = (x.hi & M) & ((x.hi >> 1) & M);
  uint128 r; r.lo = loMask; r.hi = (hiMask & 0xFFFFFFFFULL) << 32; return r;
}
```

